## Why This Is Asked
Tests budgeted DP on strings and reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][j][d] encodes a subproblem under a deletion budget; 
- palindrome subsequence logic with matches/skips; 
- reconstruction paths under deletion budget.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalWithDeletions(s, k){
  const n=s.length; 
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][i][0]=1; for(let d=1; d<=k; d++) dp[i][i][d]=1; }
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      for(let d=0; d<=k; d++){
        if(s[i]===s[j]){
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j-1][d]+2, (d>0?dp[i+1][j][d-1]:0), (d>0?dp[i][j-1][d-1]:0));
        } else {
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j][d], dp[i][j-1][d], (d>0?dp[i+1][j-1][d-1]:0));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...Array.from({length:k+1},(_,d)=>dp[0][n-1][d]));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can space be reduced to O(n^2)?
- How to reconstruct the deletion indices?
- Change objective to count distinct palindromic subsequences with budget.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP with exactly k block selection, reconstruction, and non-adjacent constraint handling. Requires precomputing interval values (max-min) and a clean end-at-i transition that accounts for the gap requirement.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP with exactly k non-adjacent blocks
- Precomputation of val(l,r) = max(A[l..r]) - min(A[l..r]) using O(n²) sliding window enumeration
- Reconstruction pointers to retrieve endpoints
- Time complexity: O(n²) precomputation + O(kn²) DP; Space: O(n²) for val table + O(n·k) for DP
- Edge cases: k > ⌊n/2⌋ (impossible, return error), empty arrays (return 0)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSumIntervals(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const val = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let min = A[l], max = A[l];
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      min = Math.min(min, A[r]);
      max = Math.max(max, A[r]);
      val[l][r] = max - min;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const prev = j >= 2 ? dp[j-2][t-1] : (t === 1 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        if (prev === -Infinity) continue;
        const curr = prev + val[j-1][i-1];
        if (curr > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = curr;
          parent[i][t] = { start: j, end: i, prev: j - 2 };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t]) {
    const p = parent[i][t];
    if (!p.skip) {
      intervals.push([p.start, p.end]);
      i = p.prev;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], intervals: intervals.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP skills with an additional dimension for tracking turns while handling obstacles and grid boundaries. It verifies understanding of initialization logic, edge cases, and how to aggregate the final answer by summing across valid turn counts.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP over cells, turn count, and last direction
- Turn counting via direction changes during transitions
- Correct handling of obstacles and grid boundaries
- Time/space trade-offs and proper base case logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, K) {
  const m = grid.length;
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[m-1][n-1] === 1) return 0;

  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [0, 0])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = 1; // right
  dp[0][0][0][1] = 1; // down

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        // from left (coming from right)
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t][0]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t-1][1]; // turn
        }
        // from top (coming from down)
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t][1]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t-1][0]; // turn
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    ans += dp[m-1][n-1][t][0] + dp[m-1][n-1][t][1];
  }
  return ans;
}
```

This runs in O(m × n × K) time and O(m × n × K) space.

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to extend classic path DP problems with an additional resource constraint, evaluating proficiency in managing 3D DP states, path reconstruction via backtracking, and space optimization.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) tracking exact t boosts used at position (i,j)
- Two transition options: boost or skip the current cell
- Path reconstruction by backtracking through the DP table
- Space optimization: reduce to two rows or maintain a compact parent pointer table

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, k, B) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(NEG))
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0] + B;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
          if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const boosts = [];
  let i = n - 1, j = m - 1, t = k;
  while (i > 0 || j > 0) {
    const fromTop = i > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j];
    const fromLeft = j > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j];
    const boostedFromTop = i > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;
    const boostedFromLeft = j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;

    if (boostedFromTop || boostedFromLeft) boosts.push([i, j]);
    if (boostedFromTop || (i > 0 && fromTop && !boostedFromTop)) { i--; }
    else if (j > 0) { j--; if (!boostedFromLeft && !fromLeft) t--; }
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n-1][m-1][k], boosts: boosts.reverse() };
}
```

## Complexity
- **Time:** O(n × m × k)
- **Space:** O(n × m × k) or O(m × k) with row optimization

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a 3D state on a grid path, a natural but less common extension of standard grid DP.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP on a grid, path constraints (only right/down), exact boosts
- Bottom-up iteration, reconstruction pointers, space/time considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBoostOnGrid(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        const fromTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t] : NEG;
        const fromLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t] : NEG;
        let best = Math.max(fromTop, fromLeft);
        if (best > NEG/2) dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
        if (t > 0) {
          const hotTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1] : NEG;
          const hotLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1] : NEG;
          const bestPrev = Math.max(hotTop, hotLeft);
          if (bestPrev > NEG/2) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], bestPrev + 2*grid[i][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space if k is large but grid values are small?
- How would you reconstruct the boosted cell coordinates?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: parity constraint on subarrays adds a practical twist to classic DP without changing core technique.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Ending a subarray with even length using prefix sums
- Path reconstruction and memory optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEvenSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let l = 2; l <= i; l += 2) {
          const val = dp[i-l][t-1] + (S[i] - S[i-l]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            choice[i][t] = l;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

